## 4. Hardware clearance and independence

HARDWARE CLEARANCE:
    methodology for removing unnecessary realization assumptions.

HOST INDEPENDENCE:
    semantic invariants do not depend on a particular software host environment
    within the qualified boundary.

HARDWARE INDEPENDENCE:
    semantic invariants survive materially different hardware realizations
    within the qualified boundary.

Neither independence is established merely by abstraction.

Both require realization evidence and preservation tests.

## 5. Canonical correction

CANONICAL does not mean primitive.

A canonical is a qualified reusable semantic relation whose invariant is stable when invoked.

A canonical may decompose into more fundamental relations.

A realization may decline/unsupported a canonical.

An extension may compose canonicals but SHALL NOT silently mutate their qualified invariants.

## 6. Orthogonal correction

An ORTHOGONAL is not merely a category.

It is a semantic dimension for which independent variation can be demonstrated while selected other material dimensions remain invariant.

Candidate roles retained for continued testing:

    identity
    position
    order
    scope

Other earlier candidates remain derived unless new evidence reverses that conclusion.

Orthogonality requires counterexample testing.

## 7. Dimensionality

DIMENSIONALITY describes the minimal independently relevant coordinates needed to qualify a semantic construct under applicability.

It is not assumed Euclidean, spatial, finite, or identical across constructs.

Dimensions SHALL be derived/tested rather than accumulated by vocabulary.

## 8. Topology

TOPOLOGY describes qualified relational structure such as:

    adjacency
    connection
    reachability
    containment
    overlap
    boundary
    path
    region

Topology SHALL NOT assume physical geometry unless the realization requires it.

## 9. Taxonomy

TAXONOMY classifies admitted structures.

Classification SHALL NOT define kernel meaning.

Where possible taxonomy should derive from semantic graph properties rather than arbitrary naming.

## 10. Ontology

MCSP ontology states what MCSP itself admits.

It SHALL NOT claim to be the complete ontology of external hardware, operating systems, drivers, applications, or the physical world.

Extension ontologies may map into MCSP semantics.

## 11. Preservation priority

The next foundational target is PRESERVATION.

TRANSFER appears to depend on preservation of material distinctions across differing conditions/relations.

Host/hardware independence also depends on preservation:

    semantic invariant
      -> realization
      -> projection
      -> preserved semantic invariant

Therefore PRESERVE must be reduced before promoting TRANSFER or similar convenience semantics.

## 12. Required corrections to qualification

Conceptual tests are evidence, but not runtime evidence.

The 0.16 conceptual 10/10 result remains a CONDITIONAL PASS only.

The 0.20 hardware-independence matrix remains architecture evidence only.

The 0.21 witness contracts remain UNVERIFIED until executed.

No file in the current lineage establishes universal hardware independence.

## 13. Completion gates

G1 Kernel reduction:
    attack PRESERVE and remaining hidden semantic residue.

G2 Self-description:
    represent the current kernel and semantic architecture in MCSP's own admitted form.

G3 Canonical normalization:
    define deterministic canonical equivalence without relying on English names.

G4 Orthogonal qualification:
    prove or reject candidate independent dimensions through controlled variation.

G5 Decomposition:
    reduce TRANSFER, READ, WRITE, COPY, ALLOCATE, RELEASE, SIGNAL and related canonicals toward the kernel.

G6 Realization mappings:
    construct explicit x86-64, AArch64, RISC-V and non-CPU mappings.

G7 Assembler witnesses:
    keep NASM and GAS representations independently reconstructible.

G8 Runtime evidence:
    build and execute qualified witnesses and compare projected observations.

G9 Driver experiment:
    express a small device/driver contract using only required MCSP kernel/canonicals/extensions.

G10 Closure:
    test whether MCSP can define and qualify its own governing structures without undefined semantic authority.

G11 Host independence:
    qualify across materially different software host environments.

G12 Hardware independence:
    qualify across materially different hardware realization classes.

## 14. Recommended order

    PRESERVE reduction
    -> canonical normalization
    -> orthogonal qualification
    -> canonical decomposition
    -> self-description encoding
    -> concrete realization witnesses
    -> runtime qualification
    -> driver experiment
    -> closure audit
    -> bounded host/hardware-independence qualification

## 15. Constructive seal

0.22 is a reconciliation checkpoint.

It does not replace or rewrite prior versions.
It records the currently preferred interpretation of their lineage.

No Permanent Seal is asserted.
